      "final_message": "I rewrote `parse_duration` in `solution.py` so it follows the README, and `python3 -m unittest -v test_public.py` passes both tests.\n\n- **How it parses:** it uses a strict full-string regex, `(?:([0-9]+)h)?(?:([0-9]+)m)?(?:([0-9]+)s)?`, and returns `h*3600 + m*60 + s` as an int.\n- **Errors:** non-string input raises `TypeError`. Anything that doesn't fully match raises `ValueError`, and so does the empty string.\n- **Other checks I ran:** `\"05m\"`\u2192300, `\"2h\"`\u21927200, `\"10s\"`\u219210 and `\"1h5s\"`\u21923605 come out right. These are all rejected: `\"\"`, `\"1m2h\"`, `\"1d\"`, `\"h\"`, `\"1h \"`, `\"+1h\"`, `\"-1s\"`, a trailing newline, and full-width digits like `\"\uff11h\"`. Only plain ASCII digits 0\u20139 are accepted.\n\nDONE: Rewrote parse_duration as a strict regex parser (h, m, s in order, each optional, at least one) that returns total seconds and raises ValueError or TypeError on bad input; public tests pass.",
